Date: Tue, 8 Jun 93 15:12:13 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Seeking Mailing List Home

I want to start a certain mailing list, but can't do so here at NASA.  So
I'm seeking a host site.  I have one offer of a site that connects to uunet
once a day, but was hoping for better.  I'd of course be willing to do the
busy work, handling -request messages forwarded to me, and logging in
remotely to add/drop folks.  The list would be unmoderated, at least at
first.

Here is a draft of a list description (suggestions/criticisms welcome):

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Welcome to "AltInt", an email list on Alternative Institutions.  This list
is solely for proposing and critiquing alternative institutions for various
walks of life.  Alternatives ways to run schools, offices, households,
conversations, countries, etc. are all fair game.

This list is open to folks from any political persuasion, but general
political flaming/discussion is forbidden.  Skip the theory and just tell
us your vision of how something could be different, and how that would
work.  Many of us are truly excited to hear about creative well-considered
suggestions, no matter what the source, but quickly bored by both
ideological is-too-is-not flaming, and partisan rah-rahs for anything
"politically correct" in some camp.

Each post starting a new thread should describe an alternative institution,
giving specifics about how it would work.  You can post your own ideas, or
review (and reference) the suggestions of others.  Replies can ask for more
detail on the mechanism, how it is envisioned to handle specific cases, or
how it is different from specific alternatives.  Followup discussion may
also suggest difficult but plausible scenarios to see how well the proposed
mechanism can handle them.

Folks might also appropriately give a SHORT description of the existing
institution that an alternative is intended to replace, of problems
perceived with that institution, and a diagnosis of their root causes.
Similarly appropriate might be SHORT summaries of evidence regarding its
feasibility, such as similar institutions in use at other times and places,
laboratory and field experiments, or abstract models.

But your tone must always be civil, and we are wary of letting the
conversation slip into general debate on politics or economics; the list
administrator will reserve the right to step in and say "that's enough".
The list is mainly for finding out about novel suggestions; political
debate can be done elsewhere.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

I expect libertarian/extropian types might be more interested in this list
that other political flavors, but if true I'd like that fact to be
demonstrated by the list, not assumed in its construction.

