I can think of an easy argument against Gott's work -- he's just plain
WRONG. His work has a statistical flaw the size of Mount Rushmore,
which is that he is assuming that all the events he is observing are
independant, which they are NOT! This is a central flaw in many abuses
of statistics.  I have been thinking of writing a paper on the whole
class of errors they seem to be so common these days.

On the other hand, some of the CAP's conclusions make considerable
sense. I don't know if they are true or false, but unlike Gott's work
there is room for them to be true.

That is why I'll dismiss Gott's stuff really fast, but find a
discussion of the CAP interesting. It isn't just that I prefer the
conclusions -- and, to tell you the truth, I have no particular wish
to be a member of the only intelligent species in our lightcone or
perhaps even the universe. I'm not nearly as strong by far in my
support of the CAP as I am of, say, the value of veganism, but it does
