> eating meat that aren't controlled by these steps and a healthy use
> of vitamin supplements?

I wrote this off the top of my head -- I suspect I can dig up a much
better list, but here are just a few of the reasons that you don't
want to be eating meat (although #1 really isn't a strike against per se.)

1) You don't need it for nutrition. You don't need the protein as you
   likely get plenty from other sources, and the only nutrient it
   supplies that you have trouble getting elsewhere is B12, which you
   need in such trace quantities that you can go half a decade without
   any at all and still run fine on internal reserves, and which you
   can get easily in any multivitamin.
2) Eating too much protein leads to a number of problems. For
   instance, in order to metabolize and excrete the excess, you end up
   using up loads of calcium, which leads to all sorts of problems
   like bone thinning. (Even though the Inuit have the worlds highest
   calcium intake they also have one of the worlds highest
   osteoporosis rates, largely thanks to their high protein
   consumption.)
3) Its very hard to actually remove enough of the fat. Lots of it is
   not in the form of neatly layered fat but marbled through the
   tissue or even part and parcel of the muscle cells. Animal cell
   membranes have a lot of lipid content too, as I recall. Even very
   lean meat is still much higher in fat than is good for you -- you
   don't find meats that are only 10% fat measured by calorie content.
4) Meat is high in several other things you don't want. For instance,
   it turns out that iron, far from being good for you, is linked to
   heart disease in excess quantities.
5) Meat is your basic zero fiber foodstuff. Its not good for you
   colon.
6) Meat is a very concentrated source of calories, which means a
   little meat and you are rather full, which discourages you from
   eating a good healthy portion of vegetables, grains, and other
   things that you should be getting at your meals.

