Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

In science, there is an important distinction between "theories" which
pull together observations, predictions, equations, etc., and
"interpretations" of those theories.

In most cases, the distinction is not too important: Newton's theory
of gravity only lends itself to one interpretation (thatI can think
of), namely, the familiar one.

Quantum mechanics, however, presents a different case. The standard
theory predicts most observed behaviors quite accurately, some to more
decimal places than any other theory yet formulated (quantum
electrodynamics). But the *interpretations* of QM are many, from the
Copenhagen Interpretation to the Many Universes Interpretation to
others.

These interpretations typically have no testable consequences. That
is, the interpretations are essentially equivalent in our world. If it
makes one feel better to believe that Schroedinger's cat is in a mixed
state of half-death and half-life (whatever that means), fine. Or if
it makes one feel better to believe the universe bifurcates into new
universes at each quantum decision, fine (whatever that really means).

The "holographic model" is really the "holographic interpretation" and
contributes essentially nothing new, from my quick read of Talbott. 

Note that the results on Bell's Theorem are consistent with all of the
interpretations of QM. 

"But what's going on, _really_?"

I suspect we'll never know, and that the question is largely
meaningless, reflecting our prejudices at the macroscopic level (where
cats are either dead or alive and are not both) and our lack of
intuition at the microscopic level.

When I was studying physics, I used to devour books on quantum
mechanics, trying to figure out what was causing the "spooky action"
that caused Einstein to remain skeptical about QM (though he
contributed a lot to it). Somewhere during my freshman year I simply
"internalized" the results and it seemed completely normal for
particles to be both waves and particles, for states to remain
correlated until measured, and for "things to be different" from the
familiar macroscopic world.

The holographic interpretation, the Ted Nelson interpretation
("everything is deeply intertwingled"), the Dancing Wu Li Masters
interpretation, the Tibetan chimes interpretation....all add nothing.

For those seeking "meaning" in the quantum world, first read the real
physics, get comfortable with it, then study the various
interpretations for philosophical enjoyment.


-Tim May

(P.S. Aharonov thinks he may be able to devise an experiment to
actually measure psi, the wave function, prior to collapse (when we
typically measure psi-squared, the probability density of the wave
function). If so, some new things may emerge. I'm staying tuned.)

